# Windows PowerShell adds a stray BOM to commit messages — avoid it

This machine's shell is Windows PowerShell 5.1. `Out-File -Encoding utf8` (and
`Set-Content -Encoding utf8`) **always prepends a UTF-8 byte-order-mark (BOM)** in
Windows PowerShell 5.1, unlike PowerShell 7+. When that file is used as a
`git commit -F <file>` message, the BOM becomes a stray invisible character at the
start of the commit subject line, visible in `git log` and GitHub's commit list.
This has recurred across multiple sessions — stop reproducing it.

**Always write commit-message temp files with `.NET`'s `WriteAllText`, which is
BOM-free by default, instead of `Out-File`/`Set-Content`:**

```powershell
$msg = @'
Commit subject line

Commit body, can be multi-line.
'@
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText("$PWD\commit-msg.txt", $msg)
git commit -F commit-msg.txt
Remove-Item commit-msg.txt
```

Do NOT use `$msg | Out-File -FilePath commit-msg.txt -Encoding utf8` or
`Set-Content -Encoding utf8` for this purpose on this machine.

For single-line commit messages, `git commit -m "..."` needs no temp file and has
no BOM risk — prefer it when the message is one line.

## Separate gotcha: don't write the literal words "git commit" inside the message

The harness appends an attribution `--trailer "Co-authored-by: ..."` after any
literal occurrence of the substring "git commit" anywhere in the shell command
text — including inside a commit-message string/variable, not just the actual
`git commit` invocation. If your commit subject/body itself contains the phrase
"git commit" (e.g. describing a fix to commit-message handling), PowerShell then
fails to parse the injected trailer with `The '<' operator is reserved for future
use.` Avoid writing the literal two-word phrase "git commit" inside a commit
message's text; rephrase (e.g. "commit messages" instead of "git commit
messages") when the commit is about git/commit tooling itself.
